WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.567
-Our next guest is
a very funny comedian,

00:00:02.567 --> 00:00:04.467
you know, from his work
on "Saturday Night Live."

00:00:04.467 --> 00:00:07.400
The 49th season of "SNL"
kicks off this Saturday,

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:10.400
October 14th on NBC
and streams live on Peacock

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.701
with host Pete Davidson
and musical guest Ice Spice.

00:00:13.701 --> 00:00:16.968
Please welcome to the show.
Marcello Hernandez, everybody.

00:00:16.968 --> 00:00:19.167
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:27.000
♪♪

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:34.934
♪♪

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:40.567
♪♪

00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:43.400
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.167
Marcello, this is, uh,

00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:46.434
I want to get this
out of the way.

00:00:46.434 --> 00:00:48.934
This is your first appearance
on a talk show.

00:00:48.934 --> 00:00:50.033
-Yes.
Can you tell?

00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:52.033
[ Laughter ]

00:00:52.033 --> 00:00:53.601
-It's just -- it's amazing that
you know

00:00:53.601 --> 00:00:58.334
all the customs --
tuxedo, corsage for the host,

00:00:58.334 --> 00:01:01.000
bottle of wine
with no glasses or corkscrew.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.501
[ Laughter ]

00:01:03.501 --> 00:01:06.434
This is so -- I'm so happy.
I really love this.

00:01:06.434 --> 00:01:07.934
I'm going to take it off
only because I'm worried

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:11.467
I'm going to poke your eye out,
but -- I talk with my hands.

00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:13.701
Congratulations on
an exceptional first season

00:01:13.701 --> 00:01:15.767
on the show.
-Oh, thank you so much.

00:01:15.767 --> 00:01:17.534
Thank you. Thank you, guys.

00:01:17.534 --> 00:01:19.100
-I hope this is true,

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.968
but when I watch
new cast members

00:01:21.968 --> 00:01:23.334
and I think
the first time

00:01:23.334 --> 00:01:25.234
I really took stock of you
was behind the Update Desk.

00:01:25.234 --> 00:01:27.133
You seemed like you were
having a very good time,

00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:29.834
which is a very hard thing to do
on a stressful show like that.

00:01:29.834 --> 00:01:31.968
But I think one of the reasons
the audience likes to see you

00:01:31.968 --> 00:01:33.667
is you seem
to be enjoying yourself.

00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:35.267
-I absolutely am.

00:01:35.267 --> 00:01:37.734
I used to sell tickets
on the street in New York,

00:01:37.734 --> 00:01:41.367
so there's nothing
to not be happy about.

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:42.868
[ Laughter ]

00:01:42.868 --> 00:01:46.501
There's no reason for me not to
be having the time of my life.

00:01:46.501 --> 00:01:48.467
-Well, it definitely shows.

00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:49.801
And it's very exciting
to have you back.

00:01:49.801 --> 00:01:53.000
And also, I was backstage.
Your mom is here tonight.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.501
-My mom is here today.

00:01:54.501 --> 00:01:55.534
-I think she is right
over there.

00:01:55.534 --> 00:01:57.400
-There she is.
Yes, that's my mom!

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:01.634
I -- can I say something
to here?

00:02:01.634 --> 00:02:03.434
-You can say whatever
you like to your mom.

00:02:03.434 --> 00:02:05.100
-Mom, very quickly,
I just want to say

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.434
I am so grateful for you

00:02:06.434 --> 00:02:07.901
and everything that
you sacrificed for me

00:02:07.901 --> 00:02:09.767
in my life
and everything I do,

00:02:09.767 --> 00:02:11.567
including this,
is always for you.

00:02:11.567 --> 00:02:14.767
[ Speaking in Spanish ]

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:15.934
-Aww.

00:02:15.934 --> 00:02:18.801
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:18.801 --> 00:02:20.667
That is so sweet.

00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:23.400
And also it's funny because
I was in the dressing room

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.067
and you walked out
and I said to your mom,

00:02:25.067 --> 00:02:26.901
You must be so proud of him.
And she went, "Eh."

00:02:26.901 --> 00:02:29.434
[ Laughter ]

00:02:29.434 --> 00:02:31.601
-That's -- that sounds like her.

00:02:31.601 --> 00:02:33.133
Yes, that sounds like her.

00:02:33.133 --> 00:02:34.400
-I'm joking, of course.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.434
But you -- you did a sketch
with Pedro Pascal...

00:02:37.434 --> 00:02:39.334
-Yes.
...who was. a really wonderful

00:02:39.334 --> 00:02:40.334
season of host last year,

00:02:40.334 --> 00:02:41.767
but he did stand out.
-Yes.

00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:44.300
-And he played
an overprotective mother.

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:47.934
And there's a Pedro
with a mustache,

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:49.067
which is very funny to me.

00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:52.534
And was this based
at all on your mom?

00:02:52.534 --> 00:02:54.767
-Not physically.

00:02:54.767 --> 00:02:56.534
-I think that is clear.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:56.534 --> 00:02:58.567
It's almost why it was good
to have a cut to

00:02:58.567 --> 00:03:00.934
of your beautiful mother, yeah.
-Thank you.

00:03:00.934 --> 00:03:03.133
He came to the show
and he kind of had this idea

00:03:03.133 --> 00:03:04.901
of wanting to do something
in Spanish,

00:03:04.901 --> 00:03:06.267
and he wanted to be my mother.

00:03:06.267 --> 00:03:09.133
It was, you know, he kind of had
this vibe coming in,

00:03:09.133 --> 00:03:11.367
and I was lucky enough to be
the only person at the show

00:03:11.367 --> 00:03:12.901
that could write in Spanish.

00:03:12.901 --> 00:03:15.534
And it just gave me
the great opportunity.

00:03:15.534 --> 00:03:18.234
So, Pedro, my second mom,
thank you. I love you.

00:03:18.234 --> 00:03:19.834
-And what are some of
the things that I know

00:03:19.834 --> 00:03:21.167
it's based on actual thing.

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:23.334
What were some things
your mom would say?

00:03:23.334 --> 00:03:25.267
-Well...
-You knew as as somebody

00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:26.701
with a sense of humor
were funny things?

00:03:26.701 --> 00:03:29.300
-Well, the great line --
one of the pieces of the sketch

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:32.767
is that my mother
doesn't believe in ADD.

00:03:32.767 --> 00:03:37.701
And so one of the lines
is my son don't have ADD.

00:03:37.701 --> 00:03:38.968
He just liked to jump.

00:03:38.968 --> 00:03:41.067
[ Laughter ]

00:03:44.734 --> 00:03:46.734
So that kind of sounds
like her.

00:03:46.734 --> 00:03:49.334
-You you took advantage
of being able to write

00:03:49.334 --> 00:03:50.801
in Spanish multiple times
last year.

00:03:50.801 --> 00:03:52.667
-Yes.
-Ana de Armas was on.

00:03:52.667 --> 00:03:54.167
-Oh, wow.
And you wrote --

00:03:54.167 --> 00:03:57.033
this is -- it was
a Spanish class.

00:03:57.033 --> 00:04:00.200
And you grew up in Miami,
but you went to college in Ohio

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:02.734
and you did a take
Spanish in college.

00:04:02.734 --> 00:04:04.701
-Yes, I took --

00:04:04.701 --> 00:04:07.033
I wanted
to feel smart in school.

00:04:07.033 --> 00:04:09.000
-Yeah.
-So I took the hardest class

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.334
that they had, you know,
Spanish one million.

00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:12.767
And it was so funny to me

00:04:12.767 --> 00:04:14.834
because the teachers
in the Spanish classes

00:04:14.834 --> 00:04:16.534
in the Midwest,
they have American names.

00:04:16.534 --> 00:04:18.601
You know, I walked in
and my teacher's name was,

00:04:18.601 --> 00:04:19.801
you know, Seth Meyers,

00:04:19.801 --> 00:04:21.968
like something like --
just like a name

00:04:21.968 --> 00:04:23.734
that you knew immediately
you were going to be like,

00:04:23.734 --> 00:04:25.400
oh, I'm better
than this person.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:27.667
[ Laughter ]

00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:28.868
And it was just shocking.

00:04:28.868 --> 00:04:30.400
Like, I remember
one of the times,

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:32.701
like the first day everybody
introduces themselves

00:04:32.701 --> 00:04:36.033
and, you know, the ladies like,
"Jake, por favor?"

00:04:36.033 --> 00:04:39.834
And he's like, "Hola,
mi nombre es Jake

00:04:39.834 --> 00:04:43.267
and yo soy from Cincinnati

00:04:43.267 --> 00:04:46.934
and mi comida
favorito es pizza.

00:04:46.934 --> 00:04:49.567
And then I'm like,
"No, now me, please, me.

00:04:49.567 --> 00:04:55.000
And I'm like...
[ Speaking in Spanish ]

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:58.567
And then the teachers just like,
"Did you guys hear that?

00:04:58.567 --> 00:05:00.234
That is your new teacher."

00:05:00.234 --> 00:05:02.834
[ Laughter ]

00:05:02.834 --> 00:05:04.167
[ Applause ]

00:05:04.167 --> 00:05:09.067
Now, you actually --
a college athlete.

00:05:09.067 --> 00:05:10.334
-Yes.
-Played soccer in college?

00:05:10.334 --> 00:05:11.834
-Yes, John Carroll University.

00:05:11.834 --> 00:05:13.400
-There you go.
I love to see this.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:15.801
And then because you did
a wonderful thing

00:05:15.801 --> 00:05:19.334
at the Update Desk
about a Latin baseball players

00:05:19.334 --> 00:05:21.334
and how they celebrate
differently,

00:05:21.334 --> 00:05:22.767
they interview differently.

00:05:22.767 --> 00:05:24.434
They have more style,
more flair.

00:05:24.434 --> 00:05:26.267
And you actually
got to participate

00:05:26.267 --> 00:05:28.133
in the All-Star game this year.

00:05:28.133 --> 00:05:29.367
-Yes.
-There you are looking like

00:05:29.367 --> 00:05:31.200
you know what you're doing.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.934
But even more impressive,
I want to show a clip

00:05:33.934 --> 00:05:36.467
because I've done -- I threw out
a first pitch once.

00:05:36.467 --> 00:05:37.968
I was so nervous.

00:05:37.968 --> 00:05:40.367
And you walking up to bat
in this celebrity

00:05:40.367 --> 00:05:43.667
all-star game
seemed very chill and loose.

00:05:43.667 --> 00:05:45.601
And I'll show it first
and then ask you a question.

00:05:45.601 --> 00:05:47.901
Let's watch Marcello
walking to the plate.

00:05:47.901 --> 00:05:49.701
-Oh.

00:05:49.701 --> 00:05:52.667
Is a New Yorker?

00:05:52.667 --> 00:05:54.701
-Wait a minute.
-It seems like he could be.

00:05:54.701 --> 00:05:57.167
[ Laughter ]

00:05:57.167 --> 00:05:59.901
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:06.767
-Yeah, I-I definitely did that.

00:06:06.767 --> 00:06:09.734
-I think my favorite, I really
enjoyed that update feature,

00:06:09.734 --> 00:06:13.167
the many observations you have
about Latin baseball players.

00:06:13.167 --> 00:06:15.100
One is just the pulling out
of the chain.

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:16.734
-Of course.
-Which is --

00:06:16.734 --> 00:06:18.634
Can you explain real quick?

00:06:18.634 --> 00:06:20.501
-I think it's just, one,
it's to be maybe

00:06:20.501 --> 00:06:21.901
a little more comfortable
out there.

00:06:21.901 --> 00:06:26.534
But they have a few chains,
and some of it is religious.

00:06:26.534 --> 00:06:28.434
You know what I mean?
They're always -- they always

00:06:28.434 --> 00:06:31.100
have like a, you know,
a crucifix or something.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:33.767
And, yeah, I think
they're just cool that way.

00:06:33.767 --> 00:06:35.434
They pull out their chain.

00:06:35.434 --> 00:06:36.667
They're like,
in the name of the father

00:06:36.667 --> 00:06:38.434
and the Son and the Holy Spirit,
they thank people.

00:06:38.434 --> 00:06:40.667
It's like such
a beautiful experience.

00:06:40.667 --> 00:06:41.901
I used to have a joke
I did on stage

00:06:41.901 --> 00:06:44.000
where the batter would be like,

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:45.501
you know,
in the name of the Father, Son,

00:06:45.501 --> 00:06:47.667
Holy Ghost, just in case I need
a little divine intervention.

00:06:47.667 --> 00:06:48.667
[ Chuckles ]

00:06:48.667 --> 00:06:51.701
[ Laughter ]

00:06:51.701 --> 00:06:54.200
And they're so ready afterwards.

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:56.667
-Did you -- when you auditioned
for SNL,

00:06:56.667 --> 00:06:58.968
were you pulling from
from old standup?

00:06:58.968 --> 00:07:01.133
Did you at least know?
Oh, I'm doing stuff

00:07:01.133 --> 00:07:02.701
that's worked on stage
and have a little bit

00:07:02.701 --> 00:07:04.133
more confidence because of that.

00:07:04.133 --> 00:07:05.667
-1,000%.

00:07:05.667 --> 00:07:09.300
A lot of my audition material
was about my mom

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:12.567
and about how
she escaped communism.

00:07:12.567 --> 00:07:15.434
And I'd be like, Mom,
"I had a bad day at school."

00:07:15.434 --> 00:07:17.167
And she'd be like,
"Oh, yeah?

00:07:17.167 --> 00:07:19.234
[ Laughter ]

00:07:21.334 --> 00:07:22.767
At the place
with the free food?

00:07:22.767 --> 00:07:24.801
That's...?"

00:07:24.801 --> 00:07:27.234
So a lot of the stuff
was kind of about that stuff.

00:07:27.234 --> 00:07:29.934
And she -- she's my inspiration,
my muse.

00:07:29.934 --> 00:07:33.767
-Yeah, well, I'll tell you,
the more you talk about her,

00:07:33.767 --> 00:07:35.300
the more it makes sense
that you look like you're having

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:37.501
so much fun on stage.
You seem very grateful.

00:07:37.501 --> 00:07:40.901
And I am very grateful
for both you and your mom

00:07:40.901 --> 00:07:42.167
for making you the guy you are

00:07:42.167 --> 00:07:43.601
because you're so much fun
to watch.

00:07:43.601 --> 00:07:45.133
Congratulations. Can't wait
to see your second season.

00:07:45.133 --> 00:07:46.133
-Thank you so much.

00:07:46.133 --> 00:07:47.434
-Thanks for being here,
Marcello.

00:07:47.434 --> 00:07:49.033
You guys, Marcello Hernandez.

00:07:49.033 --> 00:07:50.901
"SNL" returns live

00:07:50.901 --> 00:07:52.234
for its season premiere
this week

00:07:52.234 --> 00:07:54.534
with host Pete Davidson
and musical guest Ice Spice.

00:07:54.534 --> 00:07:56.167
We'll be right back
with the very funny

00:07:56.167 --> 00:07:57.267
Aparna Nancherla.

